lesson.text:
The hardest passages often describe a popular explanation, then show how later research complicated it. Questions test four things:

1. what the original story claimed;

2. what evidence supported it;

3. what later researchers found;

4. where the writer finally stands.

The writer's position is usually between the extremes: the story is neither wholly true nor wholly false. Beware of options that take one extreme, and of options that assign a later finding to the original researchers.

Hard research passages contain precise details about study design. Statements may alter one of them:

who was studied (patients with one condition, not everyone);

how many people took part;

how long the study lasted;

what the comparison group received.

An altered detail that the passage specifies differently → FALSE. A detail the passage never mentions → NOT GIVEN. Also watch for claims that turn "reported feeling better" into "was cured", which changes the kind of result.

A writer may support something only under certain conditions: "Offsets can be useful if they are verified", "provided that...", "as long as...", "only when...". Statements may: drop the condition ("offsets are useful") — usually NO if the writer stresses the condition, because the statement overgeneralises;

keep the condition — YES;

introduce a different condition — often NOT GIVEN.

Many Medium passages take a "yes, but" position: the idea is valuable, but it has been exaggerated or misapplied. Such writers produce statements of three kinds:

the core idea the writer accepts → YES;

the exaggerated version the writer rejects → NO;

details the writer doesn't evaluate → NOT GIVEN.

Keep two lists as you read: what the writer accepts and what the writer rejects.

Opinion passages often list criticisms of a policy and respond to each one. The writer's response may:

reject the criticism ("this argument does not hold"), so a statement repeating the criticism → NO;

accept it ("this is a genuine concern") → YES;

partly accept it ("there is something in this, but..."). Here you must work out which part the writer accepts.

Label each criticism in the margin with ✔, ✘ or ½.

Hard writers rarely simply support or oppose. They may:

endorse some uses (→ YES for those uses);

reject a strong claim made by enthusiasts (→ NO);

raise a concern without resolving it (→ statements claiming the writer has resolved it are NO; statements about other, unaddressed aspects are NOT GIVEN).

Watch for sentences that combine these moves: "Nudges have their place, though not the place their champions claim."

At Medium level, the same subject often appears in two or three paragraphs. For example, "coins" might be mentioned in several places. The question's type word decides which paragraph is correct:

"the origin of" → where it began;

"a disadvantage of" → a problem;

"a challenge to a common belief" → a correction of what people usually think.

Before answering, locate every paragraph that mentions the subject, then choose the one that matches the type word.

Psychology passages mix three kinds of content:

theories: explanations of why something happens ("one view is that...");

findings: results of studies ("researchers found that...");

recommendations: practical advice ("one strategy is to...").

Questions usually specify which kind they want ("a theory about...", "the result of an experiment...", "a practical suggestion..."). A paragraph that discusses the right topic but offers a different kind of content is a trap.

Research passages often describe an experiment in three parts: what was done (method), what happened (result) and what it means (interpretation). Questions may target any one of these: "a description of an experiment in which..." → method;

"a finding that..." → result;

"a suggestion about why..." → interpretation.

The three parts sometimes fall in different paragraphs.

This final Matching Information set combines the main Hard-level skills:

abstract wording;

several paragraphs on the same subject;

paragraphs with no answers;

letters used twice;

questions that target a paragraph's function (cause, evidence, criticism, proposal). For each question, decide the type word and find the sentence that fits it. Do not rely on topic matches.

This final set combines the main Hard-level skills:

the argument's structure (a popular account, then challenges, then revision);

abstract headings;

paragraphs that turn midway;

detail and keyword distractors;

headings true of the passage but not of the paragraph.

For each paragraph: (1) note its topic and function, (2) choose the heading that covers the whole paragraph, and (3) check that it is not merely a detail.

This final set brings together the Hard-level skills:

hypotheses described through several researchers;

evidence that supports one idea and undermines another;

features mentioned in more than one paragraph;

paraphrased statements about methods, claims and criticisms.

Map each hypothesis's claim, evidence and criticism before answering.

After choosing by meaning, check grammar. If the beginning ends with "so that", "in order to" or "because", only certain endings fit naturally:

in order to + base verb ("to grow food");

because + clause ("they...");

so that + subject + verb.

Grammar alone won't give you the answer, but it can quickly eliminate one or two endings.

At Hard level:

endings are abstract and heavily paraphrased ("the absence of a convincing explanation" rather than "nobody knew why lemons worked");

there are usually ten or more endings, and several are true statements from the passage that complete a different beginning;

some beginnings require you to combine information from two sentences.

Strategy: for each beginning, write a short prediction in plain words, then translate it into the more abstract language of the endings. Check the full sentence against the passage for both meaning and scope.

At Hard level:

the sentences restructure the passage's ideas, often combining two passage sentences; the correct words may be embedded in a longer phrase, and you must extract exactly the part that fits;

nearby words of the same type (other nouns, other numbers) act as distractors. After writing an answer, reread the complete sentence and ask whether it says exactly what the passage says, no more and no less.

